doi = {10.1145/3828836.3828904},
abstract = {This study examines how to design and build a flexible assembly fixture (FAF) for assembling parts of aircraft skin panels. Large, thin-walled parts with complex shapes often require more precise, flexible fixtures than traditional rigid fixtures can provide. To address these problems, this study presents a modular, adaptable fixture system that enhances the assembly process by making it more flexible, accurate, and efficient. The fixture uses the N-2-1 positioning approach to ensure everything stays in the right place and is aligned correctly. A vacuum-clamping mechanism keeps the panels in place while they are being assembled. The system was designed and tested using advanced digital technologies, including CATIA V5 for 3D modeling, DELMIA V5 for process assembly simulation, and ANSYS for finite element analysis (FEA), to assess its performance under different loads and improve it. Electric cylinder motors let you control movement very precisely, enabling the fixture to fit different panel shapes and sizes. The suggested flexible assembly fixture lowers production costs and lead times while increasing precision and adaptability by combining digital design, simulation, and control systems. This helps make digitally driven aircraft manufacturing more efficient.},
